INSERT INTO canonical_records (record_id,source_file,source_page,domain,stem,options_json,source_answer,source_explanation,provenance_json,visual_asset,visual_url,options_mode,current_answer,current_answer_status,current_evidence_json,currentness_risk,record_json,active_quiz,import_version,imported_at) VALUES ('c4dc8eaf0ee7a8733ff0','AMEDEX 2025 HEMAETOLOGY (2).pdf','70','Haematology','A 6-year-old girl with sickle cell disease is brought to your practice for routine follow-up. She is completely asymptomatic and has never had a sickle cell crisis. She takes no medications. Physical examination is unremarkable. Which one of the following complications is more likely to occur earliest in this patient?',NULL,'B','Option B Is correct

Vaso-occlusive phenomena and hemolysis are the
clinical hallmarks of sickle cell disease (SCD),
which is an inherited disorder due to homozygosity
for the abnormal hemoglobin S (HBs). Vaso-
occlusion results in recurrent painful episodes and
a variety of serious organ system complications
that can lead to lifelong disabilities and early death.
Clinical signs and symptoms typically develop at
an early age.

Bone infarction is the most common earliest
presenting symptom. This often presents as acute
pain in the hands and/or feet and is often the initial
symptom occurring in 40% of all patients in general
and 50% of children who become symptomatic
before the age of two years. The cause is bone
marrow infarction. Since pain tends to involve
bones with the highest bone marrow activity, and
because marrow activity changes with age,
different bone pain patterns are predictable. During
the first 18 months of life, the metatarsal and
metacarpals can be involved, presenting as

dactylitis or hand-foot syndrome.

An acute episode of pain is the second most
common initial presentation and the most common
symptom after the age of two years. Splenic

sequestration is the third most common prese

symptom, occurring in 20% of patients overall

one-third of Chikehitrtelwaientintiiemma@nat two years.

metacarpals can be involved, presenting as

dactylitis or hand-foot syndrome.

An acute episode of pain is the second most
common initial presentation and the most common
symptom after the age of two years. Splenic
sequestration is the third most common presenting
symptom, occurring in 20% of patients overall and
one-third of children before the age of two years.

Over time, vaso-occlusion can occur in virtually
every organ system, accounting for the
characteristic acute and chronic multisystem failure
associated with the disease.

Reference(s) * UpToDate - Overview of the clinic
al manifestations of sickle cell dise
ase

¢ Medscape - Sickle Cell Anemia

